Docker API

The Docker API (specifically the Docker Engine API) is a RESTful API exposed by the Docker daemon (dockerd) that allows you to control and automate virtually all of Docker's features using code, standard HTTP clients, or software development kits (SDKs).

Whenever you run a standard command in your terminal like docker run or docker ps, the Docker Command Line Interface (CLI) is actually translating that text into a REST API request and sending it to the Docker daemon background service to do the heavy lifting.


Core Architecture & How It Works

Docker operates on a client-server architecture:
• The Client (Docker CLI): The command-line tool you type into (docker ...).
• The Server (Docker Daemon / dockerd): The continuous background service that manages containers, images, volumes, and networks.
• The Bridge (Docker API): The communication protocol connecting them. By default, this API communicates over local, secure UNIX sockets. However, it can be exposed over a network port (TCP) if you need to manage your containers remotely.


Key Capabilities


Category	Typical Capabilities via API
Container Management	Create, start, stop, pause, kill, restart, and inspect container lifecycles.
Image Management	Pull images from registries, push custom built images, tag them, or build them from a Dockerfile.
System & Monitoring	Stream live resource usage (CPU/memory stats), grab container logs, and check system info.
Storage & Networking	Dynamically create, connect, or delete isolated networks and storage volumes.

you can use Native Language SDKs, Raw HTTP Clients (e.g., cURL), Third-Party Integrations tools like VS Code, PyCharm, and continuous integration engines (like Jenkins or GitHub Actions) tap directly into this API to build images, spin up test databases on-the-fly, or display running container metrics directly inside your editor code panel.

