Docker Auth refers to the mechanisms used to verify identity (Authentication) and control permissions (Authorization) across the Docker ecosystem. It determines who you are and what actions you are allowed to perform when interacting with Docker Registries (like Docker Hub) and the Docker Daemon API.

Because Docker operates on a client-server architecture, security is split into two primary operational areas: Registry Auth (securing images) and Daemon/API Auth (securing the runtime).

--
Docker Daemon & API Authentication (Access Control)

The Docker Daemon (dockerd) runs with root privileges on the host machine. Controlling who can send commands to this daemon is critical, as anyone with access can effectively take over the entire server.

A. Local Authentication (UNIX Sockets)

By default, the Docker CLI communicates with the Daemon via a local UNIX socket located at /var/run/docker.sock.
• Group Permissions: Local authentication relies strictly on Linux file permissions.
• Only the root user and members of the docker user group have read/write access to this socket. Adding a user to the docker group grants them full root-equivalent control over the host.

B. Remote Authentication (TLS/HTTPS)

If you need to manage a Docker server remotely over a network connection (TCP port 2376), standard password login isn't used. Instead, Docker relies on Mutual TLS (mTLS) Authentication:
• Server Verification: The Docker Daemon uses a server certificate to prove its identity to the CLI client and encrypt the traffic.
• Client Verification: The Docker Daemon requires the client to present a trusted client certificate. If the certificate is missing or not signed by the designated Certificate Authority (CA), the Daemon completely rejects the connection.
--

Docker Authorization Plugins (Fine-Grained Control)

Standard Docker authentication is all-or-nothing: if you have access to the daemon, you can run any command. To solve this, Docker supports Authorization Plugins (AuthZ).
These plugins act as a reverse proxy between the API and the Daemon. When a user sends a command, the plugin inspects the request before it executes:
• How it works: A user might be authenticated to use Docker, but the AuthZ plugin checks an internal policy database to see if they are authorized to run that specific command.
• Example Use Case: A company might implement a policy stating: "Developers are allowed to run docker ps and docker logs, but are blocked from running docker run --privileged or docker network rm."
