A Docker engine

Docker Engine is not a single program. Under the hood, it is a client-server architecture layered on top of low-level Linux Kernel features.

The Three Main User-Facing Layers

• Docker CLI (docker): The command-line client that accepts your input commands (like docker run) and translates them into instructions.
• Docker REST API: An HTTP API that the CLI uses to communicate with the background service.
• Docker Daemon (dockerd): A long-running background service that manages Docker objects—including images, containers, storage volumes, and networks.


What Actually Happens Under the Hood (Step-by-Step)

When you run a command like docker run nginx, the request travels through a modular stack of components:
1. API Call: The Docker CLI sends your command via the REST API to the Docker daemon (dockerd).
2. Image Check & Pull: dockerd checks if the image exists locally. If not, it pulls the image layers from a registry (such as Docker Hub).
3. containerd (High-Level Runtime Manager): Instead of starting the container directly, dockerd hands the request off to containerd (a CNCF-managed project). containerd manages the full container lifecycle, unpacks the image layers, and builds an OCI (Open Container Initiative) bundle.
4. runc (Low-Level OCI Runtime): containerd invokes runc, a lightweight tool built strictly to spawn containers according to the OCI specification. runc interacts directly with the Linux kernel via system calls (clone, mount, setns) to create the container, sets up isolation, launches the process, and then exits.
5. The Container Shim (containerd-shim): Once runc exits, a small process called the shim steps in to keep the container's stdin/stdout open, maintain the exit status, and prevent deadlocks.


How Isolation Works in the Kernel

A Docker container is not a mini virtual machine; it is just a regular operating system process running directly on the host kernel, wrapped with resource and visibility boundaries:
• Linux Namespaces: Control what the process can see. They isolate the process tree (PID), network interfaces (NET), mount points (MNT), IPC, and hostnames.
• Control Groups (cgroups): Control how much the process can use. They limit and account for hardware resources like CPU, memory, and I/O.
• OverlayFS (Layered Filesystem): Stacks read-only image layers (like the OS binaries and libraries) beneath a thin, read-write container layer. Any changes you make inside the container happen only in that top writable layer.
--------------

A Linux namespace is a kernel feature that isolates and partitions global system resources so that a process (or a group of processes) sees a completely different set of resources than other processes on the same machine.
In simple terms, a namespace restricts what a process can see. It tricks a program into believing it has its own dedicated copy of the operating system, making it feel like it is running entirely alone on the computer. This abstraction is the foundational technology that makes containers (like Docker, Podman, and Kubernetes) possible

